<% content_for :title, "On Psychosis | Bipolar Sober" %>

<main class="psychosis-page">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="psychosis-hero">
    <div class="psychosis-hero-content">

      <p class="psychosis-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / BIPOLAR / PSYCHOSIS
      </p>

      <p class="psychosis-hero-number">
        07 / ON PSYCHOSIS
      </p>

      <h1>
        <span>ON</span>
        <span>PSYCHOSIS<span class="psychosis-dot">.</span></span>
      </h1>

      <p class="psychosis-hero-tagline">
        WHEN YOUR MIND<br>
        BECOMES YOUR<br>
        ENTIRE REALITY.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- OPENING -->
  <section class="psychosis-opening" id="psychosis-story">
    <div class="psychosis-container">

      <p class="psychosis-label">01 / THE EXPERIENCE</p>

      <h2>
        IT WAS<br>
        <span>SPINNING</span><br>
        ON ITS OWN.
      </h2>

      <div class="psychosis-story-text">

        <p class="psychosis-opening-line">
          The record player was spinning on its own.
          <em>Round and round.</em>
        </p>

        <p>
          It was playing music on its own—a screamo rendition of
          “Amazing Grace.” <em>Interesting choice.</em>
        </p>

        <p>
          Earlier, I heard them talking about me and narrating my every
          move from outside the building. The voices stopped when I read
          a book, so I ran downstairs to the front door and read from one.
          They stopped.
        </p>

        <p>
          They were knocking on doors, asking if my neighbors would
          participate in an experiment. I opened my door and saw nobody
          in the hallway.
        </p>

        <p>
          Then they were watching me. They were still narrating everything
          I was doing. I could hear their voices as if they were in my
          apartment with me. I checked my apartment for cameras and
          microphones as they mocked me, laughing at my efforts to find
          out where this was coming from. I checked the chimney, the
          windows, the kitchen, the bedroom, the closet. Under the bed.
          Nothing. Just laughter. “She’s looking, but she’ll never find us!”
        </p>

      </div>
    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 911 -->
  <section class="psychosis-scene psychosis-scene-pink">
    <div class="psychosis-container">

      <p class="psychosis-label">02 / THE CALL</p>

      <h2>911.</h2>

      <div class="psychosis-dialogue">

        <p class="psychosis-dialogue-label">
          ME / ON THE PHONE
        </p>

        <p class="psychosis-dialogue-big">
          “THEY’RE WATCHING ME.<br>
          THEY’RE TALKING ABOUT ME.<br>
          THEY’RE IN MY APARTMENT!”
        </p>

        <p class="psychosis-dialogue-small">
          “Who?”
        </p>

        <p class="psychosis-dialogue-answer">
          “...They!!”
        </p>

      </div>

      <div class="psychosis-story-text">
        <p>
          A police officer showed up at my door. How he got into the
          building, I had no idea. He asked me if I wanted to leave with
          him. I knew I would be arrested, never to see Banjo, my family,
          or my friends ever again—to be locked away for a crime I didn’t
          even know I had committed. What else could have warranted this?
          I would have gone away to jail, wouldn’t I?
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- THE NIGHT -->
  <section class="psychosis-scene psychosis-scene-dark">
    <div class="psychosis-container">

      <p class="psychosis-label">03 / THE NIGHT</p>

      <h2>
        NOTHING<br>
        <span>WAS REAL.</span><br>
        EVERYTHING<br>
        FELT REAL.
      </h2>

      <div class="psychosis-story-text">

        <p>
          Sweat came out of every pore. Music played from my white noise
          machine. Things on my wall morphed into terrifying faces.
          Curtains moved in the wind even though the windows were locked
          tight. The city outside was blank and irrelevant—the voices
          were the only thing in focus. My brain was swimming.
        </p>

        <p>
          The four of them threw around ideas about how to torture me,
          taking turns poking my skin with hot knives and laughing at my
          pain. They sang and talked about what was happening as if they
          were explaining it scientifically. One of them started moving
          my phone off my bed, just as they had moved my vape. They had
          already moved shoes across my floor. What was one more thing?
        </p>

        <p>
          Their leader said, “She needs a job. Leave that. What is a
          chemist without their chemistry set?” One of them was named
          David. He was the least experienced. His knives hurt the least,
          but they were still fiery hot.
        </p>

      </div>

      <blockquote class="psychosis-pullquote">
        “SHE NEEDS A JOB.<br>
        LEAVE THAT.<br>
        WHAT IS A CHEMIST<br>
        WITHOUT THEIR<br>
        CHEMISTRY SET?”
      </blockquote>

      <div class="psychosis-story-text">

        <p>
          It smelled of sulfur. I sat in a puddle of my own sweat. They
          communicated with me through a Bluetooth headset, saying,
          “Follow our instructions no matter what. We will know if you
          don’t.”
        </p>

        <p>
          “How?” I asked.
        </p>

        <p>
          “We can see everything you do. We are everywhere. We can walk
          through walls. We’re invisible. We can read your thoughts.”
        </p>

        <p>
          “That’s impossible,” I said.
        </p>

        <p>
          “We implanted a chip in your brain while you slept, you fucking
          idiot!” They laughed at me.
        </p>

        <p>
          They said they were from the Canadian government, and I was
          being punished for a DUI—even though I hadn’t been to Canada
          in years.
        </p>

        <p>
          They pushed their knives into me for hours.
        </p>

        <p>
          I watched a sweatshirt move itself into the shape of a dog.
          I was sweating as they beat drums from a speaker in my bedroom.
          Terrified to watch something move on its own, I hit it with a
          wrench.
        </p>

        <p>
          “You’ll pay for that,” they said.
        </p>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- SLEEP -->
  <section class="psychosis-sleep">
    <div class="psychosis-container">

      <p class="psychosis-label">04 / NO SLEEP</p>

      <p class="psychosis-sleep-lead">
        THREE DAYS.
      </p>

      <h2>
        NO<br>
        <span>SLEEP.</span>
      </h2>

      <div class="psychosis-story-text">

        <p>
          By sunrise, they hadn’t let up. At 8 a.m., they said their shift
          was over and the day shift would take over. I’d barely slept in
          three days. By then, I was so exhausted I passed out for a few
          hours.
        </p>

        <p>
          Eventually, I began to wake up and tried to live as normally
          as I could. I started applying for jobs while two women from
          the previous night narrated everything I was doing, read what
          I wrote, mocked me, and spoke as if they were sending a report
          to a manager. I wondered who the manager was as I groggily
          continued through the day.
        </p>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- RECORD PLAYER -->
  <section class="psychosis-scene psychosis-scene-purple">
    <div class="psychosis-container">

      <p class="psychosis-label">05 / THE RECORD PLAYER</p>

      <div class="psychosis-command">
        <span class="psychosis-command-index">
          THE VOICE
        </span>

        <p>
          “PUT YOUR PHONE<br>
          ON THE RECORD<br>
          PLAYER.”
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="psychosis-story-text">

        <p>
          “Put your phone on the record player.” The instruction came
          clearly. “And don’t look.” I did as I was told, but watched
          in a mirror.
        </p>

      </div>

      <div class="psychosis-warning">
        “DO NOT LOOK!”
      </div>

      <div class="psychosis-story-text">

        <p>
          I didn’t look right then, but I heard a jolt-zap noise and
          turned slightly. I saw what looked like a small bolt of
          lightning come from the spinning record player and my cell
          phone.
        </p>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- REACHING OUT -->
  <section class="psychosis-scene psychosis-scene-yellow">
    <div class="psychosis-container">

      <p class="psychosis-label">06 / REACHING OUT</p>

      <h2>
        I HAD<br>
        <span>NEVER BEEN</span><br>
        MORE SCARED.
      </h2>

      <div class="psychosis-story-text">

        <p>
          Later, I remembered I had another phone. They had established
          that the bathroom was a safe zone where I had privacy. I didn’t
          think about the phone—since they could read my thoughts—and
          retrieved it without looking at it, since they could see
          through my eyes. I slipped it in my pants and went into the
          bathroom, closing the door.
        </p>

        <p>
          I texted my parents. I emailed my therapist. I had never been
          more scared in my life.
        </p>

      </div>

      <div class="psychosis-help-words">
        AN ESCAPE
        <br>WAS IN SIGHT.
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- DIAGNOSIS -->
  <section class="psychosis-diagnosis">
    <div class="psychosis-container">

      <p class="psychosis-label">07 / THE DIAGNOSIS</p>

      <div class="psychosis-diagnosis-heading">
        <span>A FEW DAYS LATER.</span>

        <h2>
          BIPOLAR I.<br>
          <span>WITH PSYCHOTIC</span><br>
          FEATURES.
        </h2>
      </div>

      <div class="psychosis-story-text">

        <p>
          A few days later, a psychiatrist diagnosed me with bipolar I
          disorder with psychotic features. I finally got some real sleep
          after getting about six hours over five days.
        </p>

      </div>

      <div class="psychosis-final-line">
        THAT WASN’T THE END OF IT THOUGH—<br>
        <span>THAT WAS JUST THE BEGINNING.</span>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- UNDERSTANDING YOUR EXPERIENCE -->
  <section class="psychosis-education" id="psychosis-understanding">
    <div class="psychosis-container">

      <p class="psychosis-label">
        08 / LIVING WITH PSYCHOSIS
      </p>

      <h2>
        WHAT THE<br>
        <span>FUCK JUST</span><br>
        HAPPENED?
      </h2>

      <p class="psychosis-education-intro">
        You experienced something terrifying. Something that felt
        completely real, even when the reality you were experiencing
        wasn’t the one everyone else was living in.
      </p>

      <article class="psychosis-article">

        <!-- 01 / REALITY -->
        <section class="psychosis-article-block">

          <p class="psychosis-article-number">
            01 / YOUR REALITY
          </p>

          <h3>IT WAS REAL TO YOU.</h3>

          <p>
            You weren’t sitting around wondering whether you were
            hallucinating. You were trying to understand what the hell
            was happening. The voices were real to you. The fear was
            real. The things you saw and felt were convincing enough
            that you reacted to them as though they were actually
            happening.
          </p>

          <p>
            That’s one of the cruelest things about psychosis. Your mind
            can create an entire explanation for what’s happening, and
            you may have no reason, in that moment, to question it.
          </p>

        </section>


        <!-- 02 / HALLUCINATIONS -->
        <section class="psychosis-article-block">

          <p class="psychosis-article-number">
            02 / HALLUCINATIONS
          </p>

          <h3>YOUR SENSES TURN AGAINST YOU.</h3>

          <p>
            You heard voices commenting on everything you did. You saw
            faces in the walls and curtains moving without wind. You
            felt sensations that seemed to confirm someone was hurting
            you.
          </p>

          <p>
            You couldn’t simply decide not to hear the voices or see
            what was happening. Your senses were giving you information
            that felt as legitimate as anything else you’d ever
            experienced. And you were expected to figure out which parts
            of it to believe.
          </p>

        </section>


        <!-- 03 / DELUSIONS -->
        <section class="psychosis-article-block">

          <p class="psychosis-article-number">
            03 / DELUSIONS
          </p>

          <h3>EVERYTHING HAD AN EXPLANATION.</h3>

          <p>
            You believed you were being watched, studied, and punished.
            You thought people could read your thoughts and see through
            your eyes. Even ordinary events became evidence that
            something terrible was happening.
          </p>

          <p>
            The frightening part wasn’t just believing these things.
            It was how everything seemed to fit together. The voices,
            the noises, the movements, and your fear all reinforced
            the same story. Questioning it wasn’t as simple as telling
            yourself to think logically.
          </p>

        </section>


        <!-- 04 / BIPOLAR -->
        <section class="psychosis-article-block">

          <p class="psychosis-article-number">
            04 / BIPOLAR DISORDER
          </p>

          <h3>THIS WAS MORE THAN A MOOD SWING.</h3>

          <p>
            Psychosis took things far beyond feeling unusually
            energized, depressed, or emotionally overwhelmed. Your
            perception of reality itself had changed.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bipolar I disorder can involve psychosis during severe mood
            episodes. That doesn’t mean every manic or depressive
            episode will look like this one. But it does mean you have
            to take changes in your sleep, thinking, and perception
            seriously.
          </p>

          <p>
            You’re not alone. Sixty-three percent of people with bipolar I
            experience psychosis.
          </p>

        </section>


        <!-- 05 / SLEEP -->
        <section class="psychosis-article-block">

          <p class="psychosis-article-number">
            05 / SLEEP
          </p>

          <h3>THREE DAYS WITHOUT SLEEP.</h3>

          <p>
            You went days without meaningful rest. By the time you
            finally slept, your mind had been running through
            experiences that were terrifying, confusing, and impossible
            to escape.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sleep matters enormously when you have bipolar disorder.
            Losing it can be an early warning sign of mania and can make
            symptoms worse. You don’t have to wait until you’re hearing
            voices or questioning reality before taking serious sleep
            disruption to your psych.
          </p>

        </section>


        <!-- 06 / AFTERMATH -->
        <section class="psychosis-article-block">

          <p class="psychosis-article-number">
            06 / THE AFTERMATH
          </p>

          <h3>AND THEN YOU HAVE TO LIVE WITH IT.</h3>

          <p>
            Eventually, you got a diagnosis. You got some sleep. You
            began to understand that what you’d experienced had a
            psychiatric explanation. But understanding what happened
            doesn’t automatically make the memories less disturbing.
          </p>

          <p>
            You can look back and recognize that the voices weren’t
            people communicating with you, that you weren’t being
            tortured, and that the elaborate explanations your mind
            created weren’t reality. That doesn’t change how frightening
            those experiences were when you lived through them.
          </p>

          <p>
            You’ve watched objects, waiting for them to move. You’ve
            listened for voices. You’ve studied shadows to see if
            they’re moving. You’ve recorded objects with your phone to
            make sure they’re sitting still. The memories don’t fade.
          </p>

          <p>
            Recovery involves more than recognizing what was real and
            what wasn’t. You also have to understand your warning signs,
            take your treatment seriously, and recognize when your
            judgment may no longer be reliable.
          </p>

          <p>
            You don’t need to turn the experience into a lesson or
            pretend it made you stronger. You can acknowledge what
            happened, accept that bipolar disorder can affect your
            perception of reality, and take practical steps to protect
            yourself if symptoms return.
          </p>

        </section>

      </article>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- END -->
  <section class="psychosis-end">
    <div class="psychosis-container">

      <p class="psychosis-end-small">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / ON PSYCHOSIS
      </p>

      <h2>
        REAL TO ME.<br>
        <span>NOT REALITY.</span>
      </h2>

